{"op": "append", "content": "[\u2139\ufe0f] [2026-02-06 08:35:25 UTC] [Agent: ArchitectAgent-ProCleanup] [Project: scribe_pro_cleanup] Architectural decision: Phase ordering rationale. P1-P3 (cleanup/logging/dead-code) are LOW risk, no dependencies, build confidence. P4-P5 (god module decomposition) are HIGH risk but must precede P6 (src/ migration) because moving 3000+ line files would create merge conflicts. P6 (packaging) requires decomposed modules. P7 (DB consolidation) needs config/paths.py from P6. P8 (Postgres) needs decomposed storage from P4. P9 (reminders) needs consolidated DB from P7. P10 (optimization) is final because it depends on stable architecture. Alternative considered: packaging first (P6 before P4/P5) -- rejected because moving monolithic files then decomposing them would be significantly more disruptive. | alternatives_rejected=[\"packaging_first\", \"parallel_tracks\"]; chosen=cleanup_first_ascending_risk; decision=phase_ordering; reasoning={\"how\": \"Analyzed dependencies between phases. P6 requires clean modules from P4/P5. P7 requires path resolution from P6. P8 requires storage structure from P4. No circular dependencies.\", \"what\": \"Considered 3 orderings: (1) cleanup-first (chosen), (2) packaging-first (rejected: moving 3000+ line files then splitting = extra disruption), (3) parallel tracks (rejected: too many integration conflicts). Chose risk-ascending then risk-descending pattern.\", \"why\": \"Phase ordering directly determines implementation risk. Wrong order causes merge conflicts, import breakage, or cascading rework.\"}; priority=low; log_type=progress; content_type=log\n", "file_path": "/home/austin/projects/MCP_SPINE/scribe_mcp/.scribe/docs/dev_plans/scribe_pro_cleanup/PROGRESS_LOG.md", "id": "2026-02-06T08:35:25.682415+00:00_d4c96e81", "timestamp": "2026-02-06T08:35:25.682519+00:00"}
{"op": "commit", "ref_id": "2026-02-06T08:35:25.682415+00:00_d4c96e81", "timestamp": "2026-02-06T08:35:25.694390+00:00"}
{"op": "append", "content": "[\u2139\ufe0f] [2026-02-06 08:35:36 UTC] [Agent: ArchitectAgent-ProCleanup] [Project: scribe_pro_cleanup] Architectural decision: God module decomposition strategy. Both sqlite.py and manage_docs.py use facade pattern (proven by v2.2 ResponseFormatter precedent). Storage decomposition: SQLiteStorage becomes thin facade in __init__.py, delegates to 8 domain modules (internals, schema, migrations, projects, entries, sessions, documents, planning, telemetry). Each function receives execute_fn/fetchall_fn as first parameter. Doc management: manage_docs() becomes action router in __init__.py, delegates to 7 action modules via ACTION_ROUTER dict. Shared modules extracted for healing, validation, utils, preflight, indexing. Alternative considered: microservice split -- rejected because these are internal modules, not services, and the facade pattern has been proven in this codebase already. | alternatives_rejected=[\"microservices\", \"mixin_classes\", \"plugin_architecture\"]; decision=decomposition_strategy; pattern=facade_with_domain_modules; reasoning={\"how\": \"Mapped all 79 sqlite methods to 9 domain modules by analyzing query patterns. Mapped 29 manage_docs functions to 12 modules by analyzing action groups. Ensured each module stays under 800 lines (target: 300-500).\", \"what\": \"Facade pattern chosen over: (1) microservices (overkill for internal modules), (2) mixin classes (creates diamond inheritance), (3) plugin architecture (over-engineered for fixed function set). Facade proven by ResponseFormatter v2.2 decomposition.\", \"why\": \"God modules (3050 and 3410 lines) are the primary technical debt. Decomposition strategy determines long-term maintainability.\"}; priority=low; log_type=progress; content_type=log\n", "file_path": "/home/austin/projects/MCP_SPINE/scribe_mcp/.scribe/docs/dev_plans/scribe_pro_cleanup/PROGRESS_LOG.md", "id": "2026-02-06T08:35:36.240361+00:00_0ea17bbb", "timestamp": "2026-02-06T08:35:36.240467+00:00"}
{"op": "commit", "ref_id": "2026-02-06T08:35:36.240361+00:00_0ea17bbb", "timestamp": "2026-02-06T08:35:36.269543+00:00"}
{"op": "append", "content": "[\u2139\ufe0f] [2026-02-06 08:35:44 UTC] [Agent: ArchitectAgent-ProCleanup] [Project: scribe_pro_cleanup] Architectural decision: Path resolution strategy. Chose importlib.resources + environment variable overrides over raw __file__. config/paths.py provides 8 canonical path functions: package_root(), config_data_dir(), templates_dir(), db_init_sql(), user_data_dir(), default_db_path(), scribe_dir(), repo_root(). Each checks environment variable first (SCRIBE_DATA_DIR, SCRIBE_DB_PATH, etc.), then falls back to importlib.resources traversable. This replaces 50+ fragile __file__ patterns found by research. Alternative: pkg_resources (deprecated), Alternative: hardcoded XDG paths (not portable). | alternatives_rejected=[\"pkg_resources\", \"hardcoded_xdg\", \"pathlib__file__\"]; decision=path_resolution; locations_affected=50+; reasoning={\"how\": \"Designed 8 path functions covering all resolution patterns found in research. Each has env var override -> importlib fallback -> error pattern.\", \"what\": \"importlib.resources chosen over pkg_resources (deprecated since Python 3.9), hardcoded XDG (not Windows-compatible), and pathlib.Path(__file__) (breaks in installed packages). Env var overrides enable deployment flexibility.\", \"why\": \"50+ __file__ path resolutions are the #1 packaging risk. Must work with both pip install -e . (dev) and pip install scribe-mcp (production).\"}; priority=low; log_type=progress; content_type=log\n", "file_path": "/home/austin/projects/MCP_SPINE/scribe_mcp/.scribe/docs/dev_plans/scribe_pro_cleanup/PROGRESS_LOG.md", "id": "2026-02-06T08:35:44.500570+00:00_86204c72", "timestamp": "2026-02-06T08:35:44.500645+00:00"}
{"op": "commit", "ref_id": "2026-02-06T08:35:44.500570+00:00_86204c72", "timestamp": "2026-02-06T08:35:44.513383+00:00"}
{"op": "append", "content": "[\u2139\ufe0f] [2026-02-06 08:35:56 UTC] [Agent: ArchitectAgent-ProCleanup] [Project: scribe_pro_cleanup] Architectural decision: Database consolidation approach. Current state: 4 DB files (75MB primary + 3 redundant) + state.json (326KB) = dual source of truth. Strategy: (1) Create migration script that reads state.json and inserts into DB via StorageBackend API (upsert_project, upsert_agent_session), (2) Refactor StateManager to be DB-only (remove all JSON file I/O), (3) Fix reminders.py line 31 hardcoded path, (4) Back up legacy DBs to .scribe/backups/, (5) All paths flow through config/paths.py default_db_path(). Alternative: keep state.json as cache layer -- rejected because it creates consistency bugs and the DB already supports all needed operations. | current_dbs=4; current_state_json_size=326KB; decision=db_consolidation; reasoning={\"how\": \"Script reads state.json, maps to StorageBackend methods, inserts via API (not raw SQL). StateManager refactored to take StorageBackend in __init__. Legacy DBs backed up before deletion.\", \"what\": \"DB-only chosen over: (1) JSON cache layer (consistency bugs), (2) gradual migration (too slow, extends risk window), (3) new DB schema (unnecessary, existing schema is adequate). Migration script is idempotent.\", \"why\": \"Dual source of truth (DB + state.json) causes consistency bugs. 4 redundant DBs waste space and confuse developers.\"}; target=single_db_via_config_paths; priority=low; log_type=progress; content_type=log\n", "file_path": "/home/austin/projects/MCP_SPINE/scribe_mcp/.scribe/docs/dev_plans/scribe_pro_cleanup/PROGRESS_LOG.md", "id": "2026-02-06T08:35:56.688434+00:00_535f9866", "timestamp": "2026-02-06T08:35:56.688505+00:00"}
{"op": "commit", "ref_id": "2026-02-06T08:35:56.688434+00:00_535f9866", "timestamp": "2026-02-06T08:35:56.700232+00:00"}
{"op": "append", "content": "[\u2139\ufe0f] [2026-02-06 08:36:06 UTC] [Agent: ArchitectAgent-ProCleanup] [Project: scribe_pro_cleanup] Architectural decision: Reminder system wire-up. The reminder engine (reminders.py 418 lines + utils/reminder_engine.py 563 lines = ~1000 lines) is fully functional but only called from 2 sites in append_entry.py. Strategy: create tools/reminder_tools.py with 3 MCP tool functions -- query_reminders (read history/active), configure_reminders (modify settings per project), reset_reminders (reset cooldowns). These delegate to existing ReminderEngine and reset_reminder_cooldowns. No new engine code needed. This restores ~2000 lines of unwired infrastructure. Alternative: delete reminder system entirely -- rejected per user decision to restore, not delete. | decision=reminder_wireup; existing_code_lines=~1000; new_tools=3; reasoning={\"how\": \"Analyzed existing ReminderEngine API. query_reminders wraps get_reminder_history(). configure_reminders wraps set_project settings. reset_reminders wraps reset_reminder_cooldowns(). All delegate to existing code.\", \"what\": \"3 MCP tools chosen as the minimal viable wire-up. Considered: (1) full reminder dashboard (over-scoped for cleanup project), (2) CLI-only (doesn't reach MCP users), (3) 3 MCP tools (minimal, covers query/configure/reset). User decision: restore, not delete.\", \"why\": \"User explicitly decided to RESTORE reminder system. 2000+ lines of working code with only 2 call sites = massive underutilization.\"}; user_decision=restore_not_delete; priority=low; log_type=progress; content_type=log\n", "file_path": "/home/austin/projects/MCP_SPINE/scribe_mcp/.scribe/docs/dev_plans/scribe_pro_cleanup/PROGRESS_LOG.md", "id": "2026-02-06T08:36:06.813829+00:00_802723a4", "timestamp": "2026-02-06T08:36:06.813901+00:00"}
{"op": "commit", "ref_id": "2026-02-06T08:36:06.813829+00:00_802723a4", "timestamp": "2026-02-06T08:36:06.825817+00:00"}
